---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import portrait from '../content/about/jackson.jpg';

const buildings = (await getCollection('buildings')).sort((a, b) => a.data.number - b.data.number);
---

<Base title="About" description="Who's behind Centone, and why it exists.">
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="hero">
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Jackson"
        widths={[240, 480]}
        sizes="(min-width: 640px) 200px, 160px"
        class="portrait"
        loading="eager"
      />
      <div>
        <h1 class="sr-only">About</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Hi, I'm Jackson. Centone is where I collect buildings worth learning from, and the ideas
          that make places like them possible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <h2>Why buildings, and why rules</h2>
    <p>
      Good buildings don't happen by accident. Whether a corner store, a rowhouse, or a small
      apartment building can exist on a given block is decided long before anyone draws it, by
      zoning codes, building codes, street standards, and the way we pay for all of it. Many of the
      neighborhoods people love most would be illegal to build in most American cities today.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm interested in both halves of that: the buildings themselves, room by room, and the rules
      that decide whether we get more of them.
    </p>

    <h2>What's here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/">Buildings</a>. A growing catalog of houses I admire, each with an isometric
        drawing, scaled floor plans, and a 3D model you can spin. So far:{' '}
        {
          buildings.map((b, i) => (
            <>
              <a href={`/buildings/${b.id}/`}>{b.data.name}</a> ({b.data.location})
              {i < buildings.length - 2 ? ', ' : i === buildings.length - 2 ? ', and ' : '.'}
            </>
          ))
        }
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a>. The policy changes that would make well-designed places
        normal again, organized by category, from zoning and building codes to parking, transit,
        and finance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/knowledge/">Knowledge</a>. Essays on the ideas behind the roadmap, with sources.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I believe</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Streets are a city's largest public space, and they should be designed first for the
        people walking, cycling, and living along them.
      </li>
      <li>A car should be an option, not a requirement for everyday life.</li>
      <li>
        Neighborhoods should be allowed to grow gradually, one small building at a time, the way
        the best ones always have.
      </li>
      <li>Good design shouldn't be a luxury. The rules should make it the default.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Wider than the default reading column, and left-aligned with the other pages. */
  article.prose {
    max-width: 58rem;
    margin-left: 0;
  }

  .hero {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .portrait {
    flex: none;
    width: 200px;
    height: 200px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* The page title stays for screen readers but isn't shown. */
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 1.25rem;
    }

    .portrait {
      width: 160px;
      height: 160px;
    }
  }
</style>
